WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:06.218
This is the technical deep dive for Book
15 of the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:06.218 --> 00:00:09.187
Compliance Automation
and Day-2 Operations.

4
00:00:09.187 --> 00:00:12.578
It's for help desk leads,
cloud and platform engineers,

5
00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:17.663
and the people who automate controls: the
teams who run the estate after cutover.

6
00:00:17.663 --> 00:00:21.916
Part 1 is the front door and the
authority: how a request comes in,

7
00:00:21.916 --> 00:00:26.251
gets routed and verified, and what
the help desk may do in Entra ID.

8
00:00:26.251 --> 00:00:31.788
Part 2 is catalogs, records and controls:
typed requests, SaaS integrations,

9
00:00:31.788 --> 00:00:37.325
the CMDB, and control automation across
four platforms, then who runs it all.

10
00:00:37.325 --> 00:00:40.400
Let's start with why
day two matters so much.

11
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.537
Here's why this matters.

12
00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:45.374
A modernized estate
isn't judged at cutover;

13
00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:48.525
it's judged the first Monday
someone can't sign in.

14
00:00:48.525 --> 00:00:52.738
And the most dangerous routine action
on the help desk is a single button.

15
00:00:52.738 --> 00:00:56.981
Requiring a user to re-register
for MFA wipes their methods,

16
00:00:56.981 --> 00:00:59.925
and whoever signs in
next sets up new ones.

17
00:00:59.925 --> 00:01:04.362
So a reset done on an unverified
request doesn't fix an account;

18
00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:06.363
it hands it to whoever asked.

19
00:01:06.363 --> 00:01:10.450
Attackers know this, and they call
help desks to do exactly that.

20
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.509
Federal guidance agrees:
recovery is a bypass,

21
00:01:13.509 --> 00:01:17.213
and it needs high friction and
a notice to the real owner.

22
00:01:17.213 --> 00:01:20.975
So this book treats every
reset as an account transfer.

23
00:01:20.975 --> 00:01:23.900
Next, the model that makes that workable.

24
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:29.479
Every day-two request has to answer
three questions before anything changes:

25
00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:33.100
where does it go, who may say
yes, and what proves it happened.

26
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.844
The book answers them with
platforms the agency already runs:

27
00:01:36.844 --> 00:01:39.340
the service management platform routes,

28
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.038
the identity governance tool approves,
and Entra ID is where the change lands.

29
00:01:44.038 --> 00:01:48.182
Underneath sits a contract, not a
product: every task has an owner,

30
00:01:48.182 --> 00:01:50.684
every change has an approval on record,

31
00:01:50.684 --> 00:01:55.688
automation runs under a narrow identity,
and evidence comes out the other end.

32
00:01:55.688 --> 00:01:59.287
The five rules along the
bottom apply to every request.

33
00:01:59.287 --> 00:02:03.801
If any one is missing, the change may
be right, but it can't be attested.

34
00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:06.250
Module 1 starts at the front door.

35
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.654
Module 1 is intake, triage and routing:

36
00:02:09.654 --> 00:02:13.850
the everyday mechanics that
make the authority model real.

37
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:17.920
An estate with a clean role model
still slides backward if the team

38
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.063
keeps answering email, or if a manager's
'just do it' counts as approval.

39
00:02:22.063 --> 00:02:24.360
So we start with how work comes in.

40
00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.435
The rule is simple: one front door.

41
00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:30.860
Nothing is handled as ad
hoc email to the cloud team.

42
00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.274
The team mailbox gets a mail-flow rule that
redirects into the ticketing platform,

43
00:02:36.274 --> 00:02:41.210
and the detail matters: a blind copy
leaves two queues, a redirect leaves one.

44
00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:45.335
Run the rule in audit mode
first, compare, then enforce.

45
00:02:45.335 --> 00:02:50.453
New mail becomes a triage record with nobody
assigned, and a reroute rule pulls back

46
00:02:50.453 --> 00:02:55.423
anything sent straight to cloud services,
because old habits survive a redirect.

47
00:02:55.423 --> 00:02:59.561
Cloud services is the escalation
tier, not a second inbox.

48
00:02:59.561 --> 00:03:04.347
Approvals carry due dates that reject,
so silence never grants access.

49
00:03:04.347 --> 00:03:08.270
Once a request is in, triage
decides where it goes.

50
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.057
Triage asks one question: what
authority does this task need?

51
00:03:13.057 --> 00:03:17.908
Not who received it, not how urgent the
caller sounds, and not how senior they are.

52
00:03:17.908 --> 00:03:21.336
The checks run in a fixed order,
and the first match wins,

53
00:03:21.336 --> 00:03:26.516
so the dangerous cases are caught first:
anything claiming to come from Microsoft,

54
00:03:26.516 --> 00:03:28.954
and any target that holds an admin role,

55
00:03:28.954 --> 00:03:34.058
because a role that can reset ordinary
users can't safely reset an administrator.

56
00:03:34.058 --> 00:03:36.057
One rule deserves emphasis.

57
00:03:36.057 --> 00:03:40.557
Nothing is approved from an email,
even a convincing one from Microsoft.

58
00:03:40.557 --> 00:03:43.794
A sender address is a
quick filter, not proof.

59
00:03:43.794 --> 00:03:48.782
The check that counts is the tenant's own
audit log, matched to an approved ticket.

60
00:03:48.782 --> 00:03:51.800
Next, what automation can actually carry.

61
00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:57.109
Wherever a connector action exists, the
ticket should authorize the machine,

62
00:03:57.109 --> 00:03:59.388
and the person's role stops at approval.

63
00:03:59.388 --> 00:04:01.788
But check what the
connector really documents.

64
00:04:01.788 --> 00:04:04.787
The book checked ServiceNow's
Entra ID spoke and

65
00:04:04.787 --> 00:04:07.712
found the older design
had mapped too much to it.

66
00:04:07.712 --> 00:04:11.376
Its password reset works
only for cloud-only users.

67
00:04:11.376 --> 00:04:15.843
There's no action at all for an MFA
reset, so that stays a person's task,

68
00:04:15.843 --> 00:04:19.662
which makes verification the
control that matters most.

69
00:04:19.662 --> 00:04:22.900
And the connection type decides
which actions run at all,

70
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.275
because some of them work only
with delegated permissions.

71
00:04:26.275 --> 00:04:30.550
Coverage changes by release, so validate
it against what you've installed.

72
00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.120
Which brings us to verifying the person.

73
00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.295
Approval answers whether
something should be done.

74
00:04:36.295 --> 00:04:39.495
It doesn't answer whether the
caller is who they claim to be,

75
00:04:39.495 --> 00:04:41.495
and that's the gap attackers exploit.

76
00:04:41.495 --> 00:04:44.317
So the book builds a verification ladder,

77
00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:47.470
and it's the CISO's decision,
not the help desk's.

78
00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.919
If someone can still sign in with
a phishing-resistant method,

79
00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:52.745
that session proves who they are.

80
00:04:52.745 --> 00:04:56.540
If they've lost everything, it
takes real identity proofing,

81
00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:01.420
in person or through Entra's new account
recovery, followed by a one-time pass.

82
00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:07.220
What never counts: email, caller ID,
knowledge questions or a manager's say-so.

83
00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:11.382
The ticket records which rung was
used, never the evidence itself.

84
00:05:11.382 --> 00:05:13.820
Then the ticket has to close properly.

85
00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:17.407
A ticket closed with 'done'
is just an assertion.

86
00:05:17.407 --> 00:05:21.537
The book wants three records that
agree: the ticket shows the decision,

87
00:05:21.537 --> 00:05:26.021
the elevation record shows the privilege,
and the audit log shows the change.

88
00:05:26.021 --> 00:05:29.958
Two gaps have to be built, because
Entra won't close them for you.

89
00:05:29.958 --> 00:05:34.480
Privileged Identity Management asks for
a ticket number but never checks it,

90
00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.296
so a detection rule has to match
every activation to a real ticket.

91
00:05:38.296 --> 00:05:42.545
And Entra keeps its logs for only
thirty days, so they go to the SIEM.

92
00:05:42.545 --> 00:05:45.810
The service levels on the
right are program defaults,

93
00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:48.258
and a shorter outside clock always wins.

94
00:05:48.258 --> 00:05:51.880
Module 2 turns to the
roles behind all this.

95
00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:56.117
Module 2 is help desk
authority in Entra ID.

96
00:05:56.117 --> 00:05:57.369
In Active Directory,

97
00:05:57.369 --> 00:06:01.343
the directory tree answered whose
password the desk could reset.

98
00:06:01.343 --> 00:06:06.374
Entra answers it in three parts: the role
decides what a technician can do, the

99
00:06:06.374 --> 00:06:08.690
target decides whether it applies,

100
00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:12.205
and administrative units
(AUs) narrow the population.

101
00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:17.020
Get any one wrong, and the desk either
can't work or can do far too much.

102
00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.250
Five built-in roles carry
most help desk identity work,

103
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.570
and all five are labeled privileged.

104
00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:27.407
The column people get wrong is
whose account each role can touch.

105
00:06:27.407 --> 00:06:30.969
Most can act only on users with
no admin role, and members of

106
00:06:30.969 --> 00:06:35.420
role-assignable groups are protected
from everyone except the top tier.

107
00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.226
That protection is deliberate:

108
00:06:37.226 --> 00:06:41.045
whoever took over a member's account
would inherit the group's role.

109
00:06:41.045 --> 00:06:44.308
The other lesson is that
capability isn't authority.

110
00:06:44.308 --> 00:06:46.674
Some of these roles can delete users,

111
00:06:46.674 --> 00:06:50.145
and one can grant consent,
which the desk must never do.

112
00:06:50.145 --> 00:06:54.668
So the platform's limits aren't the
policy; the ticket and monitoring enforce

113
00:06:54.668 --> 00:06:59.120
the rest, and the desk gets a narrow
custom role for app assignments instead.

114
00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:00.720
Next, scope.

115
00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.545
Scope is what keeps those roles narrow.

116
00:07:03.545 --> 00:07:05.991
Help desk roles are assigned over AUs,

117
00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:09.857
and at that scope they reach
only members with no admin role.

118
00:07:09.857 --> 00:07:14.176
Executives go further, into a
restricted unit that only a small,

119
00:07:14.176 --> 00:07:19.158
approved support group can touch; even
tenant-wide admins can't edit them.

120
00:07:19.158 --> 00:07:22.291
One caution: don't put the
technicians themselves in one,

121
00:07:22.291 --> 00:07:25.083
because governance features
stop working there.

122
00:07:25.083 --> 00:07:29.310
Roles reach technicians through one
group per role and scope, eligible

123
00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:33.895
rather than active, and activated with
a justification that cites the ticket.

124
00:07:33.895 --> 00:07:36.420
Those groups also protect their members,

125
00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:40.283
so one technician can't reset
another and inherit their access.

126
00:07:40.283 --> 00:07:43.480
Now, recovering someone
who's lost everything.

127
00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:47.164
When a user has lost
their only strong method,

128
00:07:47.164 --> 00:07:52.318
re-registration alone leaves a gap: whoever
signs in next sets up the new method.

129
00:07:52.318 --> 00:07:55.192
A Temporary Access Pass closes that gap.

130
00:07:55.192 --> 00:07:59.006
The sequence on the slide is the
heart of it: re-proof the person,

131
00:07:59.006 --> 00:08:03.718
revoke their sessions, remove the lost
method, then issue a one-time pass with

132
00:08:03.718 --> 00:08:08.430
the shortest lifetime the handover
allows, straight to the verified person.

133
00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.686
Stay with them while they register
something phishing-resistant, then tell

134
00:08:12.686 --> 00:08:16.942
them on another channel, so a victim
learns about a reset they never asked for.

135
00:08:16.942 --> 00:08:20.068
The table shows the pass's
own limits worth knowing.

136
00:08:20.068 --> 00:08:23.700
Some tasks, though, need
more than one approver.

137
00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:29.428
Some tasks are high-risk because one person
could use them to take over an identity,

138
00:08:29.428 --> 00:08:33.600
break its link to the source of truth,
or open an app to the wrong people.

139
00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.683
For those, the book requires two approvers
besides the person doing the work,

140
00:08:38.683 --> 00:08:41.587
both recorded before
any role is activated.

141
00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:45.266
The second approver checks that
the requester was verified,

142
00:08:45.266 --> 00:08:48.650
the change is exactly specified,
and the urgency is real.

143
00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:53.541
ImmutableID repair is the telling
example: it left the help desk entirely,

144
00:08:53.541 --> 00:08:57.950
because the right fix is usually in
Active Directory, not the cloud.

145
00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:01.300
And three detection rules
watch everything the desk does.

146
00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:02.670
That's Part 1.

147
00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:08.420
Part 2 begins with Module 3:
catalogs, SaaS and the CMDB.

148
00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.460
Hundreds of identity changes a
week, each done as a portal click,

149
00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:15.645
are the biggest steady
source of control erosion.

150
00:09:15.645 --> 00:09:20.503
The answer is a catalog of typed
requests, plus two things it leans on:

151
00:09:20.503 --> 00:09:24.600
governed SaaS integrations
and a CMDB you can trust.

152
00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:30.476
Every catalog item runs the same loop:
a typed request against a named target,

153
00:09:30.476 --> 00:09:34.951
the approval its control needs,
execution under a narrow identity,

154
00:09:34.951 --> 00:09:39.100
and a record that closes only
when a check confirms the change.

155
00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:43.241
Every identity change is one
of five verbs: add, move,

156
00:09:43.241 --> 00:09:47.875
change, delete or reset, and
each has its own closing check.

157
00:09:47.875 --> 00:09:50.400
The one that fails audits is the leaver.

158
00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.807
Disabling the account isn't enough;
sessions, connected applications

159
00:09:54.807 --> 00:09:59.062
and everything the person owned all
have to be handled and recorded.

160
00:09:59.062 --> 00:10:02.583
Apps without automatic
provisioning become manual tasks,

161
00:10:02.583 --> 00:10:05.888
with no automatic check that
the account is really gone.

162
00:10:05.888 --> 00:10:09.590
Telephony has its own
catalog, with real stakes.

163
00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:15.018
Teams Phone is where a silent configuration
error has physical consequences:

164
00:10:15.018 --> 00:10:18.977
a wrong emergency address sends
responders to the wrong building.

165
00:10:18.977 --> 00:10:22.599
And the federal rules bind
whoever operates the phone system,

166
00:10:22.599 --> 00:10:24.090
which includes an agency.

167
00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:29.646
Kari's Law requires direct 911 dialing and
a notice to someone such as a front desk,

168
00:10:29.646 --> 00:10:34.290
and the FCC's rules require a
dispatchable location with every call.

169
00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:36.068
Teams provides the mechanisms,

170
00:10:36.068 --> 00:10:39.215
but someone has to configure
them and keep them current.

171
00:10:39.215 --> 00:10:43.196
So a validated address becomes
a gate on every number,

172
00:10:43.196 --> 00:10:48.715
network changes raise their own mapping
item, and a 933 test call closes it.

173
00:10:48.715 --> 00:10:52.803
Counsel decides compliance;
the catalog keeps the evidence.

174
00:10:52.803 --> 00:10:54.460
Next, SaaS.

175
00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:59.742
An administrator can connect a third-party
SaaS service in about fifteen minutes,

176
00:10:59.742 --> 00:11:02.407
and nothing along the
vendor's path asks whether

177
00:11:02.407 --> 00:11:05.073
that service is authorized
to hold federal data.

178
00:11:05.073 --> 00:11:06.735
That's the point of this slide.

179
00:11:06.735 --> 00:11:09.874
Consent controls decide who
may approve an integration;

180
00:11:09.874 --> 00:11:13.347
they don't decide whether the
service should get the data at all.

181
00:11:13.347 --> 00:11:18.611
So the book puts a gate first: no
authorization verdict, no configuration.

182
00:11:18.611 --> 00:11:23.542
Its default for identity data is a FedRAMP
certification at Class C or higher,

183
00:11:23.542 --> 00:11:26.085
read from the Marketplace's own dataset.

184
00:11:26.085 --> 00:11:30.835
Name matching only ever creates a
review queue, never an automatic no.

185
00:11:30.835 --> 00:11:34.510
Then the question is how to
configure it with least privilege.

186
00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:38.708
The catalog configures integrations
through Microsoft Graph,

187
00:11:38.708 --> 00:11:43.198
and the book checked the older design's
permissions against Microsoft's reference.

188
00:11:43.198 --> 00:11:46.159
The operations were right;
the permissions weren't.

189
00:11:46.159 --> 00:11:50.868
One narrow permission, limited to applications
the connector owns, covers almost

190
00:11:50.868 --> 00:11:53.776
everything, and adding
the broad synchronization

191
00:11:53.776 --> 00:11:55.922
permission would undo that boundary.

192
00:11:55.922 --> 00:12:00.796
The real trap is assignment: the permission
it needs can grant anything to anyone,

193
00:12:00.796 --> 00:12:04.434
which turns the connector into
a privileged administrator.

194
00:12:04.434 --> 00:12:06.445
So it stays off the connector,

195
00:12:06.445 --> 00:12:10.885
and a person binds the access group
once, under a just-in-time role.

196
00:12:10.885 --> 00:12:14.185
After that, access changes
are just group membership.

197
00:12:14.185 --> 00:12:19.810
Every integration also has to land
correctly in the CMDB, so that's next.

198
00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.444
Every ticket names an asset,

199
00:12:22.444 --> 00:12:26.622
so the help desk can only be as
right as the inventory behind it.

200
00:12:26.622 --> 00:12:31.772
The book's position is clear: the CMDB
is a projection, not the authority.

201
00:12:31.772 --> 00:12:36.814
Discovery and connectors fill it, but
identity comes from the naming plane,

202
00:12:36.814 --> 00:12:39.660
the IP address management and DNS records.

203
00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.513
ServiceNow's engine
already decides whether a

204
00:12:42.513 --> 00:12:45.585
record is new and which
source may write each field.

205
00:12:45.585 --> 00:12:50.082
The book adds one more pass: match
each asset to the naming plane,

206
00:12:50.082 --> 00:12:54.060
bind that identity, and flag
anything that doesn't match.

207
00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.006
A shadow asset is a finding,

208
00:12:56.006 --> 00:12:59.985
and a gap gets fixed upstream,
never patched in the CMDB.

209
00:12:59.985 --> 00:13:02.470
Then come the everyday defects.

210
00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:06.758
This slide shows why CMDB
hygiene isn't a nicety.

211
00:13:06.758 --> 00:13:10.264
A missing, duplicate or stale
record breaks patching and

212
00:13:10.264 --> 00:13:13.994
evidence in different ways, and
the table walks through each.

213
00:13:13.994 --> 00:13:16.997
Duplicates come from
the sources themselves,

214
00:13:16.997 --> 00:13:19.683
from reimaged devices to merged records.

215
00:13:19.683 --> 00:13:23.700
The rule is to bind, not delete:
keep the record that carries the

216
00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:28.270
authoritative identity, and move
its tickets and findings over first.

217
00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:32.747
Stale records follow a sequence:
detect, have the owner confirm,

218
00:13:32.747 --> 00:13:37.483
retire, and only then delete, because
some deletions can't be undone.

219
00:13:37.483 --> 00:13:42.307
A deleted device, for example, takes
its BitLocker recovery keys with it.

220
00:13:42.307 --> 00:13:44.171
That closes Module 3.

221
00:13:44.171 --> 00:13:47.410
Module 4 turns to control automation.

222
00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:51.435
Module 4 is control
automation, and who runs it.

223
00:13:51.435 --> 00:13:55.747
The vendors already test most of
the settings an assessor asks about.

224
00:13:55.747 --> 00:14:00.195
The gap is everything around the
test: who owns the finding, what clock

225
00:14:00.195 --> 00:14:05.160
they work against, who approved the
fix, and what re-test proved it closed.

226
00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:11.321
The book runs one loop on every platform:
test with the native tool, detect drift,

227
00:14:11.321 --> 00:14:16.648
raise a typed task against the asset, fix
it natively, and close on the next test.

228
00:14:16.648 --> 00:14:18.209
Two rules matter most.

229
00:14:18.209 --> 00:14:22.396
The ticketing platform reads
control state but never writes it,

230
00:14:22.396 --> 00:14:24.923
and a task closes on a
re-test, not a click.

231
00:14:24.923 --> 00:14:28.679
On Microsoft 365, CISA's
ScubaGear is the main test,

232
00:14:28.679 --> 00:14:34.135
and its stable policy IDs let the mapping
from failure to task live as data.

233
00:14:34.135 --> 00:14:37.431
Conditional Access exceptions
get the same treatment,

234
00:14:37.431 --> 00:14:39.223
with an approver and an expiry.

235
00:14:39.223 --> 00:14:41.088
Compliance Manager is useful,

236
00:14:41.088 --> 00:14:44.372
but it's a readiness signal,
not the evidence of record.

237
00:14:44.372 --> 00:14:47.990
Azure works the same way,
with its own caveats.

238
00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:53.828
Azure ships a lot of help here, and Microsoft
is unusually candid about its limits.

239
00:14:53.828 --> 00:14:57.861
Azure Policy's FedRAMP initiative
reports compliant only for the

240
00:14:57.861 --> 00:15:02.041
settings it can test, which is a partial
view, not a verdict on a control.

241
00:15:02.041 --> 00:15:06.740
Results arrive on a daily cycle, so
closures lag the fix by up to a day.

242
00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:09.413
Exceptions have a native
form with an expiry,

243
00:15:09.413 --> 00:15:12.665
and the book allows them only
from an approved request.

244
00:15:12.665 --> 00:15:16.142
Defender for Cloud adds
dashboards and exports,

245
00:15:16.142 --> 00:15:20.140
but an export only fires on
change, so silence isn't proof.

246
00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:22.877
Resource Graph is the simple read path.

247
00:15:22.877 --> 00:15:25.420
So how do results reach the ticket queue?

248
00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:30.580
There are three routes from a native
result into the ticketing platform: the

249
00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:35.270
vendor's own connector, the platform's
import, or the program's read-only collector.

250
00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:39.066
Which one you can use depends on
your cloud; the Defender connector,

251
00:15:39.066 --> 00:15:41.895
for instance, isn't available
in Azure Government.

252
00:15:41.895 --> 00:15:44.400
The important rule is
one route per source,

253
00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:48.695
because two routes give you two tasks
and two clocks for the same finding.

254
00:15:48.695 --> 00:15:50.470
And the clock itself matters.

255
00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:55.706
For vulnerabilities it's CISA's new
directive on prioritizing security updates,

256
00:15:55.706 --> 00:15:59.864
set by exposure and known
exploitation, not FedRAMP's timeframes,

257
00:15:59.864 --> 00:16:02.482
which bind cloud providers, not agencies.

258
00:16:02.482 --> 00:16:06.620
Next, AWS, where the
ground shifted this year.

259
00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:11.570
On AWS, the evidence path has
changed under people's feet.

260
00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:16.114
Config conformance packs and Security
Hub's posture checks are the foundation,

261
00:16:16.114 --> 00:16:19.832
and both run in GovCloud, though
with differences you need to test.

262
00:16:19.832 --> 00:16:24.757
Security Hub keeps findings for only
ninety days, so copy them out first.

263
00:16:24.757 --> 00:16:29.941
The big correction is Audit Manager: it
closed to new customers in April 2026,

264
00:16:29.941 --> 00:16:35.371
has no GovCloud endpoint, and its FedRAMP
framework is still on the old revision.

265
00:16:35.371 --> 00:16:38.295
Nobody should start a federal
evidence program on it.

266
00:16:38.295 --> 00:16:41.813
And the ServiceNow
connector most agencies used

267
00:16:41.813 --> 00:16:45.245
loses support in March
2027, so plan that move now.

268
00:16:45.245 --> 00:16:47.440
VMware is harder still.

269
00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:51.865
In the agency's own data center,
there's nothing to inherit.

270
00:16:51.865 --> 00:16:56.314
No provider operates the hypervisor,
no one publishes a control mapping,

271
00:16:56.314 --> 00:17:00.540
and an agent inside a virtual machine
can't see the host underneath it.

272
00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:04.065
So every piece of evidence
is the agency's to produce.

273
00:17:04.065 --> 00:17:10.130
The tools exist, but they're moving:
Broadcom replaced its compliance feature

274
00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:15.815
between versions 9.0 and 9.1, and the
new one doesn't list an 800-53 benchmark.

275
00:17:15.815 --> 00:17:20.640
Moving clusters to images unlocks both
the patch and configuration tests.

276
00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.263
And where no machine
can reach, a recorded,

277
00:17:23.263 --> 00:17:26.040
time-boxed session is
a declared exception.

278
00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:28.500
Finally, who runs all of this.

279
00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:33.562
Day two is run by the people who touch
the estate most and decide the least,

280
00:17:33.562 --> 00:17:38.162
so the design puts every decision with
someone who isn't holding the keyboard.

281
00:17:38.162 --> 00:17:40.287
The slide shows one decider per row,

282
00:17:40.287 --> 00:17:44.534
from the CISO's verification procedure
to the change advisory board,

283
00:17:44.534 --> 00:17:48.488
which authorizes changes even to
the approval rules themselves.

284
00:17:48.488 --> 00:17:51.975
Only the authorizing official
accepts open-ended risk.

285
00:17:51.975 --> 00:17:54.276
The separations on the
right are the ones the

286
00:17:54.276 --> 00:17:57.012
platform should enforce
rather than leave to memory.

287
00:17:57.012 --> 00:17:59.568
Above all, nobody approves their own work,

288
00:17:59.568 --> 00:18:02.424
and nobody changes the
gate they pass through.

289
00:18:02.424 --> 00:18:05.780
Contractors can prepare every
decision here except the

290
00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:09.063
authorizing official's, which
is a government-only role.

291
00:18:09.063 --> 00:18:11.120
So, what to decide first?

292
00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:16.045
Six decisions, each with a named
owner, and the table shows who.

293
00:18:16.045 --> 00:18:20.661
Two stand out: the verification ladder,
because it protects every reset,

294
00:18:20.661 --> 00:18:24.807
and the SaaS threshold, because
it's a gate nothing else replaces.

295
00:18:24.807 --> 00:18:28.508
The first step needs no procurement:
measure before you build.

296
00:18:28.508 --> 00:18:33.352
Run the read-only inventories and collectors
against a test scope, size the work

297
00:18:33.352 --> 00:18:38.196
from what they find, and start the move
off the AWS connector before its deadline.

298
00:18:38.196 --> 00:18:41.507
Then build only what the native
tools don't already provide.

299
00:18:41.507 --> 00:18:46.721
Operations is where modernization either
becomes routine or quietly reverts.

300
00:18:46.721 --> 00:18:50.345
The book has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

301
00:18:50.345 --> 00:18:51.160
Thank you.
